This is to address #40 

As is the norm with this sort of stuff, it took a bit of R&D, so I am doing a mini write up before the full PR:

I came up with this scheme of passing strings between a WASM host and Rust and wanted to bounce it off with the team before proceeding further. The idea is that all CLI arg parsing goes through Rust, but obviously, we need Node to run `.wx` files. So when someone executes `wing run blah.wx`, it goes to Rust. Rust does its thing if it needs to (sanitation, parsing, etc.) and then Rust fills a buffer in WASM's linear memory, and after Rust returns, WASM host (in this case: Node) can consume that buffer.

The buffer and its length are declared as statics and therefore are guaranteed to live in the linear memory region of WASM. `.as_mut_ptr()` (look at the diff) essentially returns the offset of the static in the linear region.

This is sample code to consume the buffer in a WASM host:

```JS
// call to Rust through WASM, passing argv and argc to it
// wait for Rust to do its thing...
// Rust returns here and we can do:
const linearMemory = instance.exports.memory;
const offset = instance.exports.get_executable_buf();
const stringBuffer = new Uint8Array(linearMemory.buffer, offset,
  instance.exports.get_executable_len());

let str = '';
for (let i=0; i<stringBuffer.length; i++) {
  str += String.fromCharCode(stringBuffer[i]);
}

console.log(str);
```

This makes `wing run <blah>` a no-op in native builds and WASM builds "trap" the run call and can do whatever afterwards.

The alternative would've been defining an `extern` function which is to be imported from the WASM host in runtime, but that breaks native builds as there's no formal definition of that `extern` function in non WASM builds.

